# 🚀 Salesforce Bulkification, SOQL & DML Best Practices

## 📖 Introduction

When Salesforce executes a Trigger or Apex class, it may process **1 record, 50 records, or even 200 records** in a single transaction.

As Salesforce developers, we should **never write code that only works for one record**.

Instead, our code should efficiently handle **multiple records** while staying within Salesforce Governor Limits.

---

# 🔍 Why is SOQL Inside a Loop Dangerous?

## ❌ Incorrect Approach

```apex
for (Application__c app : Trigger.new) {

    Student__c student = [
        SELECT Id, Name, CGPA__c
        FROM Student__c
        WHERE Id = :app.Student__c
    ];

}
```

## What happens?

Imagine Salesforce sends **200 Application records**.

```
200 Applications
        │
        ▼
200 SOQL Queries
        │
        ▼
Governor Limit Exception
```

Salesforce allows only **100 SOQL Queries** in one transaction.

So after the 101st query, Salesforce throws the error:

```
Too many SOQL queries: 101
```

---

## ✅ Correct Approach

### Step 1 – Collect all Student IDs

```apex
Set<Id> studentIds = new Set<Id>();

for(Application__c app : Trigger.new){
    studentIds.add(app.Student__c);
}
```

### Step 2 – Retrieve all Students in one query

```apex
Map<Id, Student__c> students = new Map<Id, Student__c>([
    SELECT Id, Name, CGPA__c
    FROM Student__c
    WHERE Id IN :studentIds
]);
```

### Step 3 – Use the Map inside the loop

```apex
for(Application__c app : Trigger.new){

    Student__c student = students.get(app.Student__c);

    System.debug(student.Name);

}
```

## ✅ Why is this better?

Only **one SOQL query** is executed, no matter whether Salesforce processes:

- 1 record
- 50 records
- 200 records

This makes the code **bulk-safe**.

---

# ⚠️ Why is DML Inside a Loop Dangerous?

## ❌ Incorrect Approach

```apex
for(Application__c app : Trigger.new){

    app.Status__c = 'Eligible';

    update app;

}
```

## What happens?

Suppose Salesforce processes **200 records**.

```
200 Applications
        │
        ▼
200 Update Statements
        │
        ▼
Governor Limit Exception
```

Salesforce allows only **150 DML statements** in one transaction.

---

# ✅ Correct Approach

Create a list first.

```apex
List<Application__c> applicationsToUpdate = new List<Application__c>();

for(Application__c app : Trigger.new){

    app.Status__c = 'Eligible';

    applicationsToUpdate.add(app);

}
```

Update all records together.

```apex
update applicationsToUpdate;
```

## ✅ Why is this better?

Instead of

```
update
update
update
update
```

Salesforce performs

```
One Update

↓

All Records Updated
```

This saves resources and follows Salesforce best practices.

---

# 🚀 What is Bulkification?

Bulkification means writing Apex code that works efficiently for **multiple records** instead of just one.

A bulkified program should work correctly whether Salesforce processes:

- 1 record
- 10 records
- 50 records
- 200 records

without changing the code.

---

# 📊 Bulkification Process

```
Receive Trigger Records
          │
          ▼
Collect Required IDs
          │
          ▼
Execute One SOQL Query
          │
          ▼
Store Results in a Map
          │
          ▼
Process All Records
          │
          ▼
Collect Records to Update
          │
          ▼
Perform One DML Operation
```

---

# 📦 Why Do We Use Collections?

## List

A **List** stores multiple records.

```apex
List<Application__c> applications = Trigger.new;
```

Use List when you need to process many records.

---

## Set

A **Set** stores only unique values.

```apex
Set<Id> studentIds = new Set<Id>();
```

If the same Student ID appears multiple times, the Set stores it only once.

---

## Map

A **Map** stores data as **Key → Value**.

```apex
Map<Id, Student__c> students;
```

Example

```
Student Id
      │
      ▼
Student Record
```

Instead of querying Salesforce repeatedly, we retrieve the student directly from the Map.

---

# 🎯 Advantages of Bulkification

✅ Reduces SOQL Queries

✅ Reduces DML Statements

✅ Improves Performance

✅ Processes Large Data Volumes

✅ Prevents Governor Limit Exceptions

✅ Makes Code Scalable

✅ Follows Salesforce Best Practices

---

# 💡 Interview Tip

When asked:

**Why is SOQL inside a loop dangerous?**

A good answer is:

> "Because Salesforce may process many records in one transaction. If a SOQL query runs inside a loop, one query is executed for every record, which can exceed Governor Limits. Instead, I collect record IDs, execute one bulk SOQL query outside the loop, store the results in a Map, and process the records using that Map."

---

# 📝 Key Takeaways

✅ Always assume Salesforce can process multiple records.

✅ Never write SOQL queries inside loops.

✅ Never perform DML operations inside loops.

✅ Use Lists to store records.

✅ Use Sets to store unique values.

✅ Use Maps for fast record lookup.

✅ Query once, process many.

✅ Update once, save many.

---

# 📚 Conclusion

Bulkification is one of the most important concepts in Salesforce development.

Writing bulk-safe Apex helps applications perform efficiently, prevents Governor Limit errors, and ensures that the same code works correctly whether Salesforce processes **1 record or 200 records**.
